<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Housecarl</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as húskarl, huscarl</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A housecarl (Old Norse húskarl, 'house man'; Old English huscarl) was a non-servile manservant or household bodyguard in medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-europe">Northern Europe</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> the word first meant any free manservant and came to mean a lord's retainer; the institution was brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> by the Danish conquest in the 11th century, where a special tax paid the royal housecarls in coin. They also served as royal agents: in 1041 two of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harthacnut">Harthacnut</a>'s housecarls, collecting a heavy levy, were killed in a revolt at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worcester">Worcester</a>. Historians debate whether they formed a standing elite army; under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-godwinson">Harold Godwinson</a> they were the backbone of the English army at Hastings and fought on after his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/housecarl.jpg" alt="Housecarl" width="330" height="230" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hexateuch_king.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In Old Norse a húskarl was a manservant as opposed to the húsbóndi, the master of the house. Housecarls were free men, not to be confused with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrall">thralls</a>, and Icelandic laws also call them einhleypingar and lausamenn, men not tied, stressing that they served of their own will. With time the word came to mean a retainer in a lord's hirð or bodyguard; the Turinge stone speaks of housecarls raising a memorial to a commander who fell in Garðar, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><p>Danish runestones use the related word heimþegi, a man given a home in return for service. At Hedeby King Svein Forkbeard raised a stone in memory of his housecarl Skardi, and Johannes Brøndsted suggested that the garrison of the fort of Trelleborg may have been made up of royal housecarls. Such retainers still served Niels of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> early in the 12th century, but by its end they had probably become a new kind of nobility living away from the court.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>According to the 12th-century Danish historian Svend Aggesen, the housecarls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cnut-the-great">Cnut the Great</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> were governed by a law, the Witherlogh. They sat at the king's table by skill in war and birth, and could be moved to a lower place for minor offences; killing a fellow housecarl brought outlawry and exile, treason death. Their pay was monthly, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxo-grammaticus">Saxo Grammaticus</a>, and they could leave the king's service on only one day of the year, New Year's Eve. Domesday Book records just thirty-three landholding housecarls in 1066, most with small estates.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Cnut is said to have kept 3,000 to 4,000 men in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> as his bodyguard, and some historians see the housecarls as a disciplined standing army, a view Charles Oman put forward in 1885. Nicholas Hooper argued instead that it was time to debunk the housecarl: they were retainers with land or pay, hard to tell apart from thegns. Sceptics also note that no such army was used against the revolt of 1051. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayeux-tapestry">Bayeux Tapestry</a> shows them as footmen in mail with conical nasal helmets, fighting with great two-handed axes.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Housecarl” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Anglo-Saxon kings relied on the fyrd, the shire levies, supported by fortified burhs and, under Cnut, a paid household of <b>housecarls</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Such a retainer was a <b>housecarl</b> (húskarl) or a drengr, a “warrior”; the skald Sigvatr Þórðarson called the retainers of Olaf II of Norway heiðþegar, “receivers of pay”.</blockquote><a href="/societies/vikings">Social Hierarchy of the Vikings</a></li></ul></section>
